      {
        title: "1. AWS Global Infrastructure and Workload Boundaries",
        explanation:
          "An AWS Region is a separate geographic area containing multiple Availability Zones. An Availability Zone is an isolated location made up of one or more data centres with independent power, networking and facilities. A VPC is a logically isolated network that you define inside a Region. Subnets belong to a single Availability Zone, so a resilient design normally places equivalent public and private subnets in at least two Availability Zones. This separation helps reduce the impact of a single-zone failure, but it does not automatically make an application highly available. Compute capacity, load balancer targets, database configuration, dependencies and deployment procedures must also support the design.",
        responsibilities: [
          "Region: geographic and service deployment boundary.",
          "Availability Zone: fault-isolation boundary within a Region.",
          "VPC: private network boundary for a workload.",
          "Subnet: IP address range associated with one Availability Zone.",
          "Security group: stateful virtual firewall attached to supported resources."
        ],
        implementation: [
          "Choose a Region based on latency, service availability, compliance and cost.",
          "Create a VPC CIDR range that leaves room for future subnets and connected networks.",
          "Create equivalent subnet tiers across multiple Availability Zones.",
          "Document CIDR allocations before connecting VPNs, peering or Transit Gateway networks."
        ],
        productionConsiderations: [
          "Avoid overlapping CIDR ranges across connected VPCs and on-premises networks.",
          "Do not assume that placing an EC2 instance in a VPC makes it privately accessible.",
          "Review service-specific regional and Availability Zone limitations.",
          "Use infrastructure as code to keep network layouts consistent."
        ],
        failureModes: [
          "Subnet IP exhaustion prevents new interfaces or resources from being created.",
          "A single-AZ dependency creates a hidden availability bottleneck.",
          "Overlapping CIDRs prevent intended network connectivity.",
          "A Region-specific service dependency complicates disaster recovery."
