      {
        title: "5. Identity, Security and Secrets Management",
        explanation:
          "AWS security is based on layered controls and explicit identity permissions. IAM users, roles and policies determine which identities can perform actions on which resources. Workloads running on EC2 should normally obtain temporary credentials through an attached IAM role rather than storing long-lived access keys on disk. Security groups, private subnets, encryption, TLS, logging and service policies provide additional boundaries. The principle of least privilege means granting only the actions and resources required for a task. A production security design also needs a process for credential rotation, incident investigation, access review and controlled emergency access.",
        responsibilities: [
          "IAM role: temporary permissions assumed by an AWS service or trusted identity.",
          "IAM policy: defines allowed or denied actions and resources.",
          "KMS: manages cryptographic keys and key-use permissions.",
          "Secrets Manager: stores and supports rotation of application secrets.",
          "CloudTrail: records supported AWS API activity for auditing."
