        guidance: "Use Front Door when global HTTP application entry and routing are required. Use Application Gateway for regional layer-seven application delivery. Some architectures use both for distinct global and regional responsibilities."
      },
      {
        decision: "App Service, VM Scale Sets or AKS?",
        guidance: "Choose App Service for managed web application hosting, VM Scale Sets when VM-level control and scaling are needed, and AKS when Kubernetes orchestration is justified by workload and operational requirements."
      },
      {
        decision: "Service endpoint or Private Endpoint?",
        guidance: "Evaluate service-specific networking requirements. Private Endpoints provide private IP-based connectivity to supported services, but require correct DNS and network design."
      },
      {
        decision: "Single Region or multi-region?",
        guidance: "Choose multi-region only when business recovery requirements justify the added cost and operational complexity. Define data consistency, failover and failback behavior before implementation."
      }
    ],
    interviewQuestions: [
      {
        question: "What is the difference between an Azure subscription and a resource group?",
        answer: "A subscription is a major management, billing, quota and access-control boundary. A resource group is a logical container used to organize resources that share a lifecycle or management purpose. Resource groups do not automatically isolate network traffic or replace subscription governance."
      },
      {
        question: "What is a managed identity in Azure?",
        answer: "A managed identity provides an identity for an Azure workload so it can authenticate to supported services without storing long-lived credentials in application code. The identity still needs appropriate authorization, such as an Azure RBAC role or service-specific data permission."
      },
      {
        question: "What is the difference between Azure Front Door and Azure Load Balancer?",
        answer: "Azure Front Door provides global application entry and HTTP or HTTPS routing capabilities. Azure Load Balancer distributes supported network traffic at layer four. They operate at different layers and solve different traffic-management problems."
      }
    ]
  },

  "gcp-architecture": {
    title: "GCP Architecture",
    subtitle: "Designing secure, scalable and resilient workloads on Google Cloud",
    summary: "Google Cloud Platform architecture combines global infrastructure, regional and zonal resources, Virtual Private Cloud networking, Cloud Load Balancing, Compute Engine, Google Kubernetes Engine, Cloud Run, Cloud Storage, Cloud SQL, IAM, Cloud Monitoring and managed data services. A production design must explain how users enter the platform, how traffic is distributed, how workloads communicate, how identities are authorized, how data is protected, and how the system behaves during regional, zonal and dependency failures. This reference architecture follows a globally accessible application with a regional workload, private application networking, managed data services and centralized operations.",
    objectives: [
      "Understand Google Cloud organizations, folders, projects, Regions and Zones.",
      "Trace application traffic through DNS, global load balancing, serverless or container compute and managed databases.",
      "Design VPC networks, subnet ranges, firewall policies and private service connectivity.",
      "Apply IAM, service accounts, workload identity, encryption and audit logging.",
      "Plan GKE operations, autoscaling, observability, backup and disaster recovery."
    ],
    diagram: [
