      "Global external Application Load Balancer receives HTTPS traffic and applies routing policy.",
      "Cloud Armor evaluates configured edge security policies before application delivery.",
      "Google Cloud VPC provides regional subnet connectivity and controlled internal communication.",
      "GKE, Cloud Run or Compute Engine hosts application services according to workload requirements.",
      "Cloud SQL, Spanner or another managed data service stores persistent application state.",
      "Cloud Storage stores objects, artifacts, static content and backup exports.",
      "Cloud Monitoring, Cloud Logging, Cloud Audit Logs and Error Reporting support operations."
    ],
    sections: [
      {
        title: "1. Google Cloud Resource Hierarchy and Deployment Boundaries",
        explanation: "Google Cloud resources are organized through an organization, optional folders, projects and individual services. The organization represents the enterprise resource boundary when Cloud Identity or Google Workspace is integrated. Folders help apply policy and delegate administration across business units or environments. Projects provide an important boundary for APIs, billing association, quotas, IAM policy and resource organization. A project is not a network by itself: VPC networks and resources are created within projects, and shared VPC can allow service projects to use centrally managed host-project networking. Regions are geographic locations and Zones are isolated deployment locations within a Region. A resilient design must understand which services are regional, zonal or global instead of assuming every service has the same availability model.",
        responsibilities: [
          "Organization: enterprise-wide policy and governance boundary.",
          "Folder: groups projects and supports inherited policy.",
          "Project: resource, API, IAM, quota and billing organization boundary.",
          "Region: geographic deployment location for regional services.",
          "Zone: isolated location within a Region for zonal resources.",
          "Shared VPC: centralizes network ownership while allowing service projects to deploy workloads."
        ],
        implementation: [
          "Create separate projects for production, non-production, security and shared services where governance requires isolation.",
          "Enable only the APIs required by each workload project.",
          "Define folder hierarchy around operational ownership and policy inheritance.",
          "Choose Regions based on latency, compliance, service availability and recovery requirements.",
          "Use Shared VPC when central network administration and delegated application deployment are required."
        ],
        productionConsiderations: [
          "Document project ownership, billing responsibility, quota requirements and service dependencies.",
          "Test organization policies before applying them broadly because inherited restrictions can block deployments.",
          "Avoid using one unrestricted project as the boundary for every environment.",
          "Review service-specific regional, zonal and global availability characteristics.",
          "Keep emergency administrative access controlled, monitored and periodically tested."
        ],
        failureModes: [
          "An organization policy blocks a deployment or resource configuration.",
          "A project reaches a service quota and new resources cannot be provisioned.",
          "A missing API activation causes deployment failures.",
          "Incorrect Shared VPC permissions prevent service-project workloads from attaching to subnets.",
          "A zonal dependency causes workload interruption during a zone failure."
        ],
        practicalTask: "Design an organization hierarchy for development, staging and production. Identify which policies belong at organization, folder and project level. Explain how a central networking project can provide Shared VPC access to two application projects."
      },
      {
        title: "2. VPC Networking, Subnets and Private Service Access",
        explanation: "Google Cloud VPC networks are global resources, while their subnetworks are regional. This differs from cloud networking models where a virtual network is restricted to one Region. A subnet can provide IP ranges to resources in its Region, and a VPC can connect workloads across multiple Regions using the same logical network. Firewall rules are associated with the VPC and can use network tags or service accounts as targets. Routes determine packet forwarding, while Cloud NAT provides outbound connectivity for eligible resources that do not have external IP addresses. Private Google Access can allow supported Google APIs to be reached from eligible resources without assigning external IP addresses. Private Service Connect and private service access address different private connectivity patterns and should be selected based on the producer service and consumer requirements.",
        responsibilities: [
          "VPC network: global logical network boundary.",
          "Regional subnet: allocates internal IP ranges to supported resources.",
          "Firewall rules: control permitted ingress and egress traffic.",
          "Cloud Router: exchanges dynamic routes for supported hybrid connectivity designs.",
          "Cloud NAT: provides outbound NAT for resources without external IP addresses.",
          "Private Google Access: supports private access to eligible Google APIs.",
          "Private Service Connect: provides private service connectivity using supported endpoints."
        ],
        implementation: [
          "Plan non-overlapping primary and secondary subnet ranges before connecting environments.",
          "Create regional subnets close to the workloads that consume them.",
          "Use firewall rules targeted to service accounts or carefully managed network tags.",
          "Configure Cloud NAT for private workloads that require controlled outbound internet access.",
          "Enable Private Google Access where private workloads need supported Google APIs.",
          "Use Cloud Router and Cloud VPN or Cloud Interconnect for appropriate hybrid routing designs."
        ],
        productionConsiderations: [
          "VPC networks are global but subnet ranges are regional; account for this when planning IP allocation.",
          "Avoid broad ingress rules such as unrestricted access to administrative ports.",
