      {
        title: "6. IAM, Observability and Operational Governance",
        explanation: "Google Cloud IAM controls which principals can perform which actions on which resources. Principals can include users, groups, service accounts and federated identities. Roles contain permissions and can be granted at supported resource hierarchy levels. Production systems should prefer predefined or narrowly scoped custom roles over broad primitive roles. Workloads should use attached service accounts or supported federation mechanisms instead of long-lived downloaded keys. Cloud Audit Logs record supported administrative and data-access activity according to configuration and service behavior. Cloud Monitoring and Cloud Logging provide metrics, logs, dashboards, alerts and operational evidence. These capabilities are most useful when teams define service-level indicators, alert ownership, escalation paths and incident runbooks before production incidents occur.",
        responsibilities: [
          "IAM: grants permissions to principals through roles and resource policies.",
          "Service accounts: provide workload identities for supported Google Cloud access.",
          "Workload Identity Federation: enables supported external identity federation.",
          "Cloud Audit Logs: records supported administrative and data-access events.",
          "Cloud Monitoring: provides metrics, dashboards, uptime checks and alerting.",
          "Cloud Logging: centralizes logs for search, analysis and routing.",
          "Secret Manager and Cloud KMS: support secret and key-management workflows."
        ],
        implementation: [
          "Map human and workload responsibilities to dedicated groups and service identities.",
          "Grant the narrowest role at the lowest practical resource scope.",
          "Use service account impersonation or federation for controlled automation access.",
          "Enable and review the audit-log categories required by security and compliance.",
          "Create dashboards for request rate, latency, errors, saturation and dependency health.",
          "Define actionable alerts with ownership, severity and documented response steps."
        ],
        productionConsiderations: [
          "Review inherited IAM permissions and service-account key usage regularly.",
          "Protect audit-log sinks and ensure retention meets investigation requirements.",
          "Avoid alerting on every transient error; use service-level objectives and meaningful thresholds.",
          "Separate monitoring access from the ability to modify production resources.",
          "Test incident access and break-glass procedures without weakening normal controls."
        ],
        failureModes: [
          "A workload receives permission denied because its runtime identity is not the expected service account.",
          "A broad project role exposes more resources than the application requires.",
          "Audit logs are not routed or retained as expected.",
          "An alert is noisy, lacks an owner or does not identify a customer-impacting symptom.",
          "A service-account key is leaked or remains active after its intended use."
